<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canossa Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canossa Castle is a ruined fortress in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio Emilia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the seat of the house of Canossa, which held the March of Tuscany from 1027. In January 1077 Emperor Henry IV waited there for three days in the snow to obtain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>'s forgiveness, as guest of Countess Matilda. 'Going to Canossa' became a proverb for humble submission.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canossa-castle.jpg" alt="Canossa Castle" width="320" height="139" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo da Reggio · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canossa_ruins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
